# LEGACY-RME-DOCTOR-WORKSPACE-1 — permanent rules

## Discoverability

- Every PUBLISHED legacy RME PDF MUST be directly reachable from the doctor's
  handwritten/native RME workspace, **not only** from the patient-history
  timeline at the bottom of the page.
- The document rail renders ABOVE the handwriting canvas. A doctor must never
  have to scroll past the surface they are writing on to discover that the
  patient has an archived RME.
- The pre-existing patient-history presentation stays. The workspace rail is
  ADDITIVE — never a replacement, never a duplicate source of truth.

## Domain boundary (non-negotiable)

- The workspace integration is a **read/presentation surface only**.
- A legacy PDF MUST NEVER be converted into, or backfilled as, a `ClinicVisit`,
  a `MedicalRecord`, an odontogram, a prescription, an invoice, a payment, a
  `LabOrder` or a SATUSEHAT resource in order to appear in the workspace.
- Never create a shadow/placeholder native record so an existing list can
  render a legacy document. Build a mixed presentation model instead.
- Legacy archive evidence and native clinical state MUST NOT be collapsed into
  one data model.

## Presentation model

- Documents are projected into `App\Modules\MedicalRecord\Support\RmeWorkspaceDocument`
  — a non-persistent value object with an EXPLICIT `type` (`native` | `legacy`).
- Branch on `isLegacy()` / `isNative()`. NEVER infer the kind from an id range,
  an id comparison, or by parsing a label.
- The projection is PII-free: a date, a kind, short labels, a page count and an
  already-built URL. Never a KTP/NIK, never a storage path, never a hash, never
  clinical content.

## Single canonical data source

- The legacy side MUST come from `LegacyRmePatientHistoryService::publishedRecordsFor()`.
- Do NOT re-implement publication status, VOID filtering, branch filtering,
  patient binding, the feature flag, or doctor clinical scope in the workspace.
- The native side is the sheet collection the workspace already resolved. Pass
  it in; never re-query it, or the rail and the sheet navigation can drift.

## Read-only is structural

- Read-only means there is NO edit endpoint, not that a button is hidden.
- The workspace MUST NOT expose update, replace, delete, re-publish, date-edit,
  source-RM-edit, or persisted-annotation paths for legacy evidence.
- `void` remains the archive's only state change, keeps its own named
  permission, and is NOT a workspace action.
- If any mutation route could become reachable from this surface: FAIL CLOSED.

## Viewer

- Doctors MUST be able to open a legacy document and ZOOM IN, ZOOM OUT and
  ENLARGE/EXPAND it for clinical reading. Multi-page archives MUST expose every
  page, never only page 1.
- Multiple published archives are distinguishable by canonical clinical date.
- The viewer opens IN-PAGE (overlay). It MUST NOT navigate away from the
  workspace: switching to a legacy document must never silently discard unsaved
  native clinical input.
- Prefer existing browser/rasterizer capability. Do NOT add React, Vue, a large
  SPA framework, or a heavy PDF library for this viewer.

## Private storage

- Legacy PDF bytes are served ONLY through the policy-gated application routes
  (`rme.legacy-records.source`, `rme.legacy-records.pages.show`).
- No `/storage/...` path, no public disk, no predictable static URL, no direct
  nginx file path may ever expose an archive.
- Every byte request re-authorizes independently. "The link only renders for an
  authorized doctor" is NOT an authorization argument.

## Doctor authorization

- Doctor legacy reads use the canonical `DoctorPatientScopeService` plus the
  effective doctor branches. Never `doctor.branch_id == patient.branch_id`.
- **Same branch alone NEVER grants access.** A same-branch doctor with no
  treating relationship is refused the listing AND the bytes.
- Direct-object/URL manipulation (record id, patient id, page number) must fail
  closed. Out-of-scope ids 404 so existence cannot be probed.
- A VOIDed record never streams — enforced in the controller, not only the
  policy, because the single global `Gate::before` grants Super Admin every
  ability.

## Performance

- Document METADATA may load with the workspace; PDF/page BYTES must be lazy
  and on demand. Never render every archive's bytes on initial page load, and
  never embed a base64 PDF in HTML.
- Adding legacy documents must not introduce a query per document, per branch,
  or per permission check.

## Preserved foundations

Unchanged by this sprint: source-RM binding, separation of duties
(`LEGACY_RME_REQUIRE_SEPARATE_PUBLISHER=true`), `ClinicalClock` / Asia/Makassar,
the native cutoff date rule, the archive-only boundary, and steady-state
operations (`CAPABILITY=OFF`, `ADMISSION=EMPTY`, `ACTIVE_BATCH=NONE`).
`ROLL-4-WAVE-3` remains SKIPPED / NOT REQUIRED. RM 27541 remains
`DOCUMENT_NOT_ELIGIBLE`.

Published clinical evidence stays READABLE while the migration capability is
OFF. The capability gate stops new documents entering the archive; it is not a
statement that already-published evidence left the patient's history.
